public import StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.MayerVietoris.LongExactSequence

/-!
# Mayer--Vietoris for étale cohomology

Let `X` be a scheme and let `U`, `V` be open subschemes of `X`. The two inclusions
`U ⟶ U ⊔ V` and `V ⟶ U ⊔ V` are étale and jointly surjective, and the square

`U ⊓ V ⟶ U`, `U ⊓ V ⟶ V`, `U ⟶ U ⊔ V`, `V ⟶ U ⊔ V`

of objects of the small étale site of `X` is cartesian. It is therefore a Mayer--Vietoris
square for the small étale topology, and the Mayer--Vietoris machinery of a site applies to
it: the free abelian sheaves on the four objects form a short exact sequence

`0 ⟶ ℤ[U ⊓ V] ⟶ ℤ[U] ⊞ ℤ[V] ⟶ ℤ[U ⊔ V] ⟶ 0`,

and for an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site the contravariant `Ext`-sequence of that
short exact sequence is the long exact sequence

`0 ⟶ H⁰(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U ⊓ V, F) ⟶ H¹(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ ⋯`,

functorial in `F`. Taking `U ⊔ V = ⊤` gives the sequence for a scheme covered by two opens.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.Site` and `.topology`: the small étale site of `X`
  with objects and morphisms in one universe. This is the site on which the statement is made;
  see the implementation notes.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.opensToSite`: the inclusion of the lattice of open
  subschemes of `X` into that site.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.openSquare`: the square of `U ⊓ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ⊔ V`.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.square`: that square as a Mayer--Vietoris square for
  the small étale topology.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologySequence` and `.initialSequence`: the long
  exact sequence and its initial segment, the latter with the leading zero.

## Main results

* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.openSquare_isPullback`: the square is cartesian.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.openSquare_cover`: the two inclusions into `U ⊔ V`
  generate a covering sieve.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.shortExact`: the short exact sequence of abelian
  sheaves attached to the covering of `U ⊔ V` by `U` and `V`.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence`: the Mayer--Vietoris
  sequence over the objects of the small étale site is a long exact sequence, functorial in the
  abelian sheaf. Its three clauses are the three results that follow, which are stated
  separately because they are what the downstream files use. [Stacks, Tag 0A50] itself, whose
  terms are the absolute étale cohomology of the open subschemes, is this statement read through
  the comparison of `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietorisAbsolute` and is
  registered there, on
  `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologySequence_exact` and
  `.initialSequence_exact`: the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is exact, in every window and at its
  initial segment.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.delta_naturality`: the connecting morphisms are
  natural in the abelian sheaf.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.squareX₄IsTerminal` and
  `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologySupIsoH`: for `U ⊔ V = ⊤` the right-hand
  object of the square is terminal, so its cohomology is the global cohomology of `F`.

## Implementation notes

Cohomology over an object of a site is Mathlib's `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.H'`, the `Ext`-group
from the free abelian sheaf generated by that object; it is available only when the coefficient
universe is the morphism universe of the site. The objects of Mathlib's `X.Etale` live one
universe above its morphisms, and abelian sheaves on it at that morphism universe have neither
sheafification nor `Ext`. The statement is therefore made on `AsSmall` of `X.Etale` with the
transported topology, where objects and morphisms share a universe and both are available; the
comparison lemma identifies abelian sheaves there with abelian sheaves on `X.Etale`.

The cohomology groups appearing in the sequence are the cohomology of `F` over the objects
`U ⊓ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ⊔ V` of the small étale site. Their identification with the absolute
cohomology of the restriction of `F` to the small étale site of each of those open subschemes
is a separate comparison and is not proved here, except at the terminal object.

That the square is cartesian is reflected from schemes: the forgetful functor to schemes is
faithful and reflects pullbacks, and the pullback of two open subschemes of `X` inside a third
is their intersection. The covering condition is the characterisation of covering sieves of the
small étale topology by surjectivity on points, which for the two inclusions is
`U ∪ V = U ⊔ V`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A50](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A50)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Abelian AlgebraicGeometry Opposite

universe u

namespace Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris

/-! ### The small étale site in a single universe -/

/-- The small étale site of `X` with its objects and its morphisms in one and the same
universe. It is equivalent to `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Etale`. -/
abbrev Site (X : Scheme.{u}) : Type (u + 1) := AsSmall.{u + 1} X.Etale

section Site

variable (X : Scheme.{u})

/-- The equivalence of `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.Site X` with the small étale
site of `X`. -/
abbrev siteEquiv : X.Etale ≌ Site X := AsSmall.equiv

/-- Placing the small étale site in one universe is an equivalence of categories. -/
instance asSmallUp_isEquivalence :
    (AsSmall.up : X.Etale ⥤ Site X).IsEquivalence :=
  (siteEquiv X).isEquivalence_functor

/-- Reading an object of the one-universe small étale site as an object of `X.Etale` is an
equivalence of categories. -/
instance asSmallDown_isEquivalence :
    (AsSmall.down : Site X ⥤ X.Etale).IsEquivalence :=
  (siteEquiv X).isEquivalence_inverse

/-- The small étale topology of `X`, transported to
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.Site X`. -/
abbrev topology : GrothendieckTopology (Site X) :=
  (AsSmall.down : Site X ⥤ X.Etale).inducedTopology X.smallEtaleTopology

/-- Abelian sheaves on the small étale site of `X` have all `Ext`-groups: the site is small in
the coefficient universe, so the category of abelian sheaves on it is a Grothendieck abelian
category and in particular has enough injectives. -/
instance hasExt_sheaf_topology :
    HasExt.{u + 1} (Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) := by
  have : IsGrothendieckAbelian.{u + 1} (Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) :=
    inferInstance
  exact hasExt_of_enoughInjectives _

/-- The lattice of open subschemes of `X`, included into the small étale site of `X`: an open
immersion is étale. -/
def opensToSite : X.Opens ⥤ Site X := opensToEtale X ⋙ AsSmall.up

/-- The scheme underlying an object of the small étale site, as a functor. It is faithful and
reflects the limits that the Mayer--Vietoris square needs. -/
private abbrev siteToScheme : Site X ⥤ Scheme.{u} :=
  AsSmall.down ⋙ Scheme.Etale.forget X ⋙ Over.forget X

/-- An inclusion of open subschemes of `X` is a monomorphism in the small étale site: it is a
monomorphism of schemes, and the forgetful functor to schemes is faithful. -/
theorem mono_opensToSite_map {U V : X.Opens} (f : U ⟶ V) : Mono ((opensToSite X).map f) := by
  apply Functor.mono_of_mono_map (siteToScheme X)
  change Mono (X.homOfLE f.le)
  infer_instance

/-! ### The Mayer--Vietoris square of two open subschemes -/

/-- The square of open subschemes `U ⊓ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ⊔ V` of `X`, viewed in the small étale
site of `X`. -/
def openSquare (U V : X.Opens) : Square (Site X) :=
  ({ X₁ := U ⊓ V
     X₂ := U
     X₃ := V
     X₄ := U ⊔ V
     f₁₂ := homOfLE inf_le_left
     f₁₃ := homOfLE inf_le_right
     f₂₄ := homOfLE le_sup_left
     f₃₄ := homOfLE le_sup_right
     fac := Subsingleton.elim _ _ } : Square X.Opens).map (opensToSite X)

/-- The top-left object of the square is the intersection of the two opens. -/
@[simp] theorem openSquare_X₁ (U V : X.Opens) :
    (openSquare X U V).X₁ = (opensToSite X).obj (U ⊓ V) := rfl

/-- The top-right object of the square is the first of the two opens. -/
@[simp] theorem openSquare_X₂ (U V : X.Opens) :
    (openSquare X U V).X₂ = (opensToSite X).obj U := rfl

/-- The bottom-left object of the square is the second of the two opens. -/
@[simp] theorem openSquare_X₃ (U V : X.Opens) :
    (openSquare X U V).X₃ = (opensToSite X).obj V := rfl

/-- The bottom-right object of the square is the union of the two opens. -/
@[simp] theorem openSquare_X₄ (U V : X.Opens) :
    (openSquare X U V).X₄ = (opensToSite X).obj (U ⊔ V) := rfl

/-- The square of `U ⊓ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ⊔ V` is cartesian in the small étale site: the
pullback of two open subschemes of `X` over a third is their intersection, and the forgetful
functor to schemes reflects pullbacks. -/
theorem openSquare_isPullback (U V : X.Opens) : (openSquare X U V).IsPullback := by
  apply Square.IsPullback.of_map (siteToScheme X)
  change IsPullback (X.homOfLE inf_le_left) (X.homOfLE inf_le_right)
    (X.homOfLE (show U ≤ U ⊔ V from le_sup_left)) (X.homOfLE le_sup_right)
  exact isPullback_opens_inf_le _ _

/-- The inclusion of the first open into the union is a monomorphism of the site. -/
instance mono_openSquare_f₂₄ (U V : X.Opens) : Mono (openSquare X U V).f₂₄ :=
  mono_opensToSite_map X (homOfLE (show U ≤ U ⊔ V from le_sup_left))

/-- The inclusion of the second open into the union is a monomorphism of the site. -/
instance mono_openSquare_f₃₄ (U V : X.Opens) : Mono (openSquare X U V).f₃₄ :=
  mono_opensToSite_map X (homOfLE (show V ≤ U ⊔ V from le_sup_right))

/-- The sieve generated by two morphisms is carried to the sieve generated by their images by
the pushforward along a functor. -/
private theorem functorPushforward_ofTwoArrows {C D : Type*} [Category C] [Category D]
    (F : C ⥤ D) {U V W : C} (a : U ⟶ W) (b : V ⟶ W) :
    (Sieve.ofTwoArrows a b).functorPushforward F =
      Sieve.ofTwoArrows (F.map a) (F.map b) := by
  ext Y g
  simp only [Sieve.ofTwoArrows, Sieve.functorPushforward_ofArrows, Sieve.mem_ofArrows_iff]
  constructor <;> rintro ⟨i, c, hc⟩ <;> cases i <;>
    first | exact ⟨WalkingPair.left, c, hc⟩ | exact ⟨WalkingPair.right, c, hc⟩

/-- The two inclusions of `U` and `V` into `U ⊔ V` generate a covering sieve of the small
étale topology of `X`: they are étale and their images cover `U ⊔ V`. -/
private theorem ofTwoArrows_mem_smallEtaleTopology (U V : X.Opens) :
    Sieve.ofTwoArrows ((opensToEtale X).map (homOfLE (show U ≤ U ⊔ V from le_sup_left)))
        ((opensToEtale X).map (homOfLE (show V ≤ U ⊔ V from le_sup_right))) ∈
      X.smallEtaleTopology ((opensToEtale X).obj (U ⊔ V)) := by
  rw [Sieve.ofTwoArrows, AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.ofArrows_mem_smallEtaleTopology_iff]
  ext x
  simp only [Set.mem_iUnion, Set.mem_range, Set.mem_univ, iff_true]
  have key : ∀ (W : X.Opens) (hW : W ≤ U ⊔ V) (hx : x.val ∈ W),
      ((opensToEtale X).map (homOfLE hW)).left ⟨x.val, hx⟩ = x := fun W hW hx =>
    Scheme.homOfLE_apply' hW x.val hx
  rcases x.property with hx | hx
  · exact ⟨WalkingPair.left, ⟨x.val, hx⟩, key U le_sup_left hx⟩
  · exact ⟨WalkingPair.right, ⟨x.val, hx⟩, key V le_sup_right hx⟩

/-- The two inclusions of `U` and `V` into `U ⊔ V` generate a covering sieve of the small
étale topology, read on `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.Site X`. -/
theorem openSquare_cover (U V : X.Opens) :
    Sieve.ofTwoArrows (openSquare X U V).f₂₄ (openSquare X U V).f₃₄ ∈
      topology X (openSquare X U V).X₄ := by
  rw [Functor.mem_inducedTopology_iff_of_isCoverDense, functorPushforward_ofTwoArrows]
  exact ofTwoArrows_mem_smallEtaleTopology X U V

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris square of two open subschemes.** For open subschemes `U`, `V` of a
scheme `X`, the square `U ⊓ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ⊔ V` is a Mayer--Vietoris square for the small
étale topology of `X`. -/
def square (U V : X.Opens) : (topology X).MayerVietorisSquare :=
  haveI := mono_openSquare_f₂₄ X U V
  haveI := mono_openSquare_f₃₄ X U V
  GrothendieckTopology.MayerVietorisSquare.mk_of_isPullback (J := topology X)
    (openSquare X U V) (openSquare_isPullback X U V) (openSquare_cover X U V)

/-- The Mayer--Vietoris square of `U` and `V` is the square of `U ⊓ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ⊔ V`. -/
@[simp] theorem square_toSquare (U V : X.Opens) :
    (square X U V).toSquare = openSquare X U V := by
  simp [square]

/-- The top-left object of the Mayer--Vietoris square is the intersection of the two opens. -/
@[simp] theorem square_X₁ (U V : X.Opens) :
    (square X U V).X₁ = (opensToSite X).obj (U ⊓ V) := by
  rw [square_toSquare, openSquare_X₁]

/-- The top-right object of the Mayer--Vietoris square is the first of the two opens. -/
@[simp] theorem square_X₂ (U V : X.Opens) :
    (square X U V).X₂ = (opensToSite X).obj U := by
  rw [square_toSquare, openSquare_X₂]

/-- The bottom-left object of the Mayer--Vietoris square is the second of the two opens. -/
@[simp] theorem square_X₃ (U V : X.Opens) :
    (square X U V).X₃ = (opensToSite X).obj V := by
  rw [square_toSquare, openSquare_X₃]

/-- The bottom-right object of the Mayer--Vietoris square is the union of the two opens. -/
@[simp] theorem square_X₄ (U V : X.Opens) :
    (square X U V).X₄ = (opensToSite X).obj (U ⊔ V) := by
  rw [square_toSquare, openSquare_X₄]

end Site

/-! ### The long exact sequence -/

section Sequence

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} (F G : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (U V : X.Opens)

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris short exact sequence of abelian sheaves.** For open subschemes `U`,
`V` of `X`, the free abelian sheaves on the small étale site generated by `U ⊓ V`, by `U` and
`V`, and by `U ⊔ V` form a short exact sequence `0 ⟶ ℤ[U ⊓ V] ⟶ ℤ[U] ⊞ ℤ[V] ⟶ ℤ[U ⊔ V] ⟶ 0`,
whose outer maps are the difference and the sum of the restrictions. -/
theorem shortExact : (square X U V).shortComplex.ShortExact :=
  (square X U V).shortComplex_shortExact

/-- The sum of the two restriction maps `Hⁿ(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F)` in étale
cohomology. -/
abbrev toBiprod (n : ℕ) := (square X U V).toBiprod F n

/-- The difference of the two restriction maps `Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U ⊓ V, F)` in étale
cohomology. -/
abbrev fromBiprod (n : ℕ) := (square X U V).fromBiprod F n

/-- The connecting morphism `Hⁿ⁰(U ⊓ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U ⊔ V, F)` of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence in
étale cohomology, namely the Yoneda product with the extension class of
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.shortExact`. -/
abbrev delta (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) := (square X U V).δ F n₀ n₁ h

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris sequence in étale cohomology.** For open subschemes `U`, `V` of `X`,
an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site and `n₀ + 1 = n₁`, this is the six-term window

`Hⁿ⁰(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁰(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ⁰(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁰(U ⊓ V, F)
  ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ¹(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U ⊓ V, F)`

of the long exact sequence. -/
abbrev cohomologySequence (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    ComposableArrows AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} 5 :=
  (square X U V).sequence F n₀ n₁ h

/-- **Stacks 0A50.** The Mayer--Vietoris sequence in étale cohomology is exact: for open
subschemes `U`, `V` of a scheme `X` and an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site of `X`,
every six-term window of

`⋯ ⟶ Hⁿ(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U ⊓ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁺¹(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ ⋯`

is exact. -/
@[stacks 0A50 "exactness of each six-term window"]
theorem cohomologySequence_exact (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    (cohomologySequence F U V n₀ n₁ h).Exact :=
  (square X U V).sequence_exact F n₀ n₁ h

/-- The initial segment

`0 ⟶ H⁰(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U ⊓ V, F)
  ⟶ H¹(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ H¹(U, F) ⊞ H¹(V, F) ⟶ H¹(U ⊓ V, F)`

of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence in étale cohomology, with its leading zero. -/
abbrev initialSequence : ComposableArrows AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} 6 :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietorisInitialSequence (J := topology X)
    (square X U V) F

/-- **Stacks 0A50.** The Mayer--Vietoris sequence in étale cohomology is exact at its initial
segment, leading zero included: the sum of the two restriction maps
`H⁰(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F)` is injective. -/
@[stacks 0A50 "the long exact sequence, with its leading zero"]
theorem initialSequence_exact : (initialSequence F U V).Exact :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietorisInitialSequence_exact (J := topology X)
    (square X U V) F

/-- **Stacks 0A50, functoriality.** The connecting morphisms of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence
in étale cohomology are natural in the abelian sheaf. The restriction maps are natural by
construction, being the action of `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor`. -/
theorem delta_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    delta F U V n₀ n₁ h ≫
        ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n₁).map f).app
          (op (square X U V).X₄) =
      ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n₀).map f).app
          (op (square X U V).X₁) ≫ delta G U V n₀ n₁ h :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_delta_naturality (square X U V) F f n₀ n₁ h

/-- **Mayer--Vietoris for étale cohomology, over the objects of the small étale site.** For open
subschemes `U`, `V` of a scheme `X` and an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site of `X`, the
sequence

`0 ⟶ H⁰(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U ⊓ V, F)
  ⟶ H¹(U ⊔ V, F) ⟶ H¹(U, F) ⊞ H¹(V, F) ⟶ H¹(U ⊓ V, F) ⟶ ⋯`

is a long exact sequence, functorial in `F`.  That is the conjunction of the three clauses
stated here:

* its initial segment is exact, leading zero included, which is exactness at `H⁰(U ⊔ V, F)`,
  the one place no six-term window reaches;
* every six-term window of it is exact, which is exactness at `Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F)` and at
  `Hⁿ(U ⊓ V, F)` for every `n`, and at `Hⁿ(U ⊔ V, F)` for every `n ≥ 1`;
* the connecting morphisms are natural in `F`, the restriction maps being natural by
  construction, which is the functoriality the source asserts.

The first two clauses together are exactness at every term of the sequence.

The terms are the cohomology of `F` over the *objects* `U ⊓ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ⊔ V` of the small
étale site of `X`, which is not the shape in which [Stacks, Tag 0A50] states the result: the
source's terms are the absolute étale cohomology groups of the open subschemes. The
identification of the two is
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.h'OpensIsoH`, and the registration of the tag is on
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`, which is
this statement read through it; both live in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietorisAbsolute`.

The statement is made for arbitrary open subschemes `U`, `V`, so it is more general than the
source, whose hypothesis is `X = U ∪ V`: in that case `U ⊔ V = ⊤` is a terminal object of the
small étale site and the ambient terms `Hⁿ(U ⊔ V, F)` are the global étale cohomology groups
`Hⁿ(X, F)` by `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologySupIsoH`. -/
theorem mayerVietoris_longExactSequence :
    (initialSequence F U V).Exact ∧
      (∀ (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁), (cohomologySequence F U V n₀ n₁ h).Exact) ∧
      ∀ (G' : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (f : F ⟶ G') (n₀ n₁ : ℕ)
          (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁),
        delta F U V n₀ n₁ h ≫
            ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n₁).map f).app
              (op (square X U V).X₄) =
          ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n₀).map f).app
              (op (square X U V).X₁) ≫ delta G' U V n₀ n₁ h :=
  ⟨initialSequence_exact F U V, fun n₀ n₁ h => cohomologySequence_exact F U V n₀ n₁ h,
    fun G' f n₀ n₁ h => delta_naturality F G' U V f n₀ n₁ h⟩

end Sequence

/-! ### The ambient term when the two opens cover the scheme -/

section Top

variable (X : Scheme.{u})

/-- The whole of `X`, viewed as an object of the small étale site of `X` through the identity
of `X`. -/
abbrev self : Site X := AsSmall.up.obj (Scheme.Etale.mk (𝟙 X))

/-- `X` itself, viewed as an object of its own small étale site, is a terminal object: a
morphism of `X`-schemes into `X` is the structure morphism of its source. -/
def selfIsTerminal : IsTerminal (self X) := by
  have hci : MorphismProperty.ContainsIdentities (@Etale : MorphismProperty Scheme.{u}) :=
    ⟨fun _ => inferInstance⟩
  have hT : IsTerminal (Scheme.Etale.mk (𝟙 X)) := MorphismProperty.Over.mkIdTerminal _ _
  exact hT.isTerminalObj (AsSmall.up : X.Etale ⥤ Site X) _

/-- The open subscheme `⊤` of `X` is `X` itself, as an object of the small étale site. -/
def topIsoSelf : (opensToSite X).obj ⊤ ≅ self X :=
  (AsSmall.up : X.Etale ⥤ Site X).mapIso
    ((Scheme.Etale.forget X).preimageIso (Over.isoMk X.topIso (by simp [opensToEtale])))

/-- The open subscheme `⊤` of `X` is a terminal object of the small étale site of `X`. -/
def topIsTerminal : IsTerminal ((opensToSite X).obj ⊤) :=
  (selfIsTerminal X).ofIso (topIsoSelf X).symm

/-- **The ambient term of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is global étale cohomology.** When the
two opens cover `X`, so that the right-hand object of the square is `⊤`, the cohomology of `F`
over that object is the global cohomology of `F`, because `⊤` is a terminal object. -/
def cohomologyTopIsoH (F : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (n : ℕ) :
    F.H' n ((opensToSite X).obj ⊤) ≅ AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} F n) :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.TerminalCohomology.h'IsoH (topology X) F n _ (topIsTerminal X)

variable (U V : X.Opens)

/-- If the two opens cover `X`, the right-hand object of the Mayer--Vietoris square is a
terminal object of the small étale site of `X`. -/
def squareX₄IsTerminal (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) : IsTerminal (square X U V).X₄ := by
  rw [show (square X U V).X₄ = (opensToSite X).obj ⊤ by
    rw [square_toSquare, openSquare_X₄, hUV]]
  exact topIsTerminal X

/-- **Stacks 0A50, the ambient term.** When `X = U ∪ V` is a union of two open subschemes, the
terms `Hⁿ(U ⊔ V, F)` of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence are the global étale cohomology groups
`Hⁿ(X, F)` of the abelian sheaf `F`. -/
def cohomologySupIsoH (F : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (n : ℕ) :
    F.H' n (square X U V).X₄ ≅ AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} F n) :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.TerminalCohomology.h'IsoH (topology X) F n _
    (squareX₄IsTerminal X U V hUV)

end Top

end Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris

